          essay: `It seems that employers nowadays are more open to job hoppers as they realize that job hopping may be conducive to them in refreshing organizational culture and value. However, some employers are reluctant to hire job hoppers on the grounds that they need at least four to six years to demonstrate their progress and therefore, job hoppers who serve in a company for less than that duration will do no good to both their present and next employers. In my opinion, for those who are struggling for something new, they should not feel guilty about job hopping, but they should figure out what kind of job they really want before the final decision is made.
The commonly accepted wisdom is that flexibility means one can adapt well to various circumstances, so it should be highly valued. In particular, venturing on new settings is not detrimental to personal development in one’s early life. Job hopping will broaden experience, expand knowledge and enrich life. Additionally, job hopping may inspire smart decisions in choosing ideal jobs in the future. Apart from a few who are definite about their career direction at a younger age, most young adults, especially those who have just stepped out of the ivory tower, are still not quite clear about their future. Job hopping will undoubtedly help new graduates find their true self and their career path. Furthermore, those loyal employees do have strengths such as commitment, devotion and credibility to their work, but they also bear a higher risk of job burnout, mid-career crisis sabotage or severe career bottleneck, which may cause stagnation or even loss to the company.
There is no question that switching jobs should be done on the basis that employees have really acquired the skills in their present posts and need to further expand their knowledge or value. For those who frequently change jobs because they feel insecure or are sunk in apathy about the job or have difficulty in addressing relationships with colleagues, it deserves reflection.`,
